        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JEFFREY B. WALL, ESQ., Assistant to the Solicitor General, Department of Justice, Washington, D.C.; on behalf of the United States, as amicus curiae, supporting the Respondents."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2008-08-660",
      "confidence": 0.99,
      "document_hash": "8fb34607b1b77853edc490d8fd55b023f900af4092758110641fbfb584cca158",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fb34607b1b7-0000-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:17 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2008-08-660",
      "confidence": 0.99,
      "document_hash": "8fb34607b1b77853edc490d8fd55b023f900af4092758110641fbfb584cca158",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fb34607b1b7-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Why at the start? Why isn't the time the end, when we can -- the notice of appeal is to be filed after there is a judgment. Why isn't the proper time to determine number of days to appeal when the judgment is entered? And at that point,"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2008-08-660",
      "confidence": 0.99,
      "document_hash": "8fb34607b1b77853edc490d8fd55b023f900af4092758110641fbfb584cca158",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fb34607b1b7-0000-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: So that's the question, whether that's enough or whether the government's power to prevent discovery, which it can do, is that alone enough?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2008-08-660",
      "confidence": 0.99,
      "document_hash": "8fb34607b1b77853edc490d8fd55b023f900af4092758110641fbfb584cca158",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fb34607b1b7-0000-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 8,
        "start": 8
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Is there any advantage? I mean, a notice of appeal is the easiest document, so it's not a question that there's any labor involved in doing this. But is there any advantage to filing -- to taking the 60 days instead of the 30 days? Why would counsel want to take advantage of the extra 30 days? It isn't a question of a labor, having to write, like having to write a brief. What advantage would there be to taking the additional 30 days?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2008-08-660",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8fb34607b1b77853edc490d8fd55b023f900af4092758110641fbfb584cca158",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fb34607b1b7-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "There is something odd about -- plaintiffs come to the court seeking something."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Schor",
      "case_id": "2008-08-660",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8fb34607b1b77853edc490d8fd55b023f900af4092758110641fbfb584cca158",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fb34607b1b7-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I -- I don't think it's accurate to say that the government is an involuntary plaintiff, because Congress has said the United States will be a plaintiff under these circumstances and -- and in that respect Congress has spoken for the United States."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2008-08-660",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8fb34607b1b77853edc490d8fd55b023f900af4092758110641fbfb584cca158",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fb34607b1b7-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "May be for other purposes? I thought you told me before that it was for other purposes as well."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Rephen",
      "case_id": "2008-08-660",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8fb34607b1b77853edc490d8fd55b023f900af4092758110641fbfb584cca158",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fb34607b1b7-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "When the government declines to intervene in a qui tam action, it should not be deemed a party for purposes of the Rules of Procedure Rule 4."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2008-08-660",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8fb34607b1b77853edc490d8fd55b023f900af4092758110641fbfb584cca158",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fb34607b1b7-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "May I ask this question about: Are there a number of circuits that follow the 60-day rule?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2008-08-660",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8fb34607b1b77853edc490d8fd55b023f900af4092758110641fbfb584cca158",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fb34607b1b7-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: What is the government's position on that point?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2008-08-660",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8fb34607b1b77853edc490d8fd55b023f900af4092758110641fbfb584cca158",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fb34607b1b7-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOUTER: Do you take the position that without intervention though, nonetheless the government could appeal at the -- at the tail end?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Jeffrey B. Wall",
      "case_id": "2008-08-660",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8fb34607b1b77853edc490d8fd55b023f900af4092758110641fbfb584cca158",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fb34607b1b7-0002-0003",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the False Claims Act itself doesn't say anything about intervention. It doesn't say anything about 60 days. It just says the government has a right to come in and take over the action and run it and allow the relator to continue as a party."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Gideon A. Schor",
      "case_id": "2008-08-660",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8fb34607b1b77853edc490d8fd55b023f900af4092758110641fbfb584cca158",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8fb34607b1b7-0002-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 51,
        "start": 51
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the government is a party because it is named, served, and bound, and a real party in interest."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2008-08-660",
      "confidence": 0.0,
